</person-group><article-title>Extraction solvents affect anthocyanin yield, color, and profile of strawberries</article-title><source>Plants</source><year>2023</year><volume>12</volume><elocation-id>1833</elocation-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.3390/plants12091833</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">37176888</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmcid">PMC10181068</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig position="float" id="foods-13-00833-f001" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Structure of anthocyanins from purple sweetpotato. The pink shade indicates the anthocyanidin structure, the blue shade shows glycosylation by three glucose molecules, and the R2 and R3 are the positions for acylation. Modified from [<xref rid="B6-foods-13-00833" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>,<xref rid="B8-foods-13-00833" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>].</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="foods-13-00833-g001.jpg"><?image-name foods-13-00833-g001.jpg?><?image-size 61224?><?image-md5 1206a3822538bb68eea09f7e5e69dab9?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1899?><?image-original-width 3243?><?image-scaled-height 422?><?image-scaled-width 720?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/5d30/10969496/1206a3822538/foods-13-00833-g001.jpg?><?thumb-name foods-13-00833-g001.gif?><?thumb-size 6708?><?thumb-md5 4f3cf172839f15cb2e97d8d0ff14f0b8?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 80?><?thumb-scaled-width 136?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/5d30/10969496/4f3cf172839f/foods-13-00833-g001.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="foods-13-00833-f002" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Effects of pre-treatments on the anthocyanin-related properties for purple sweetpotato. (<bold>A</bold>) the color change from 0 s–20 min for newly cut sweetpotato slices; (<bold>B</bold>) the powder color; and the (<bold>C</bold>) CIEL*a*b* parameters, (<bold>D</bold>) contents of anthocyanins, total phenolics, and total flavonoids, and (<bold>E</bold>) antioxidant activities by scavenging of DPPH and ABTS of fresh slices and powder from four pre-treatments. From (<bold>C</bold>–<bold>E</bold>), F is for Fresh, D is for Dried, and SD is for Steamed and Dried with varied pre-treatments. ΔE were between “F 0 s” and other samples. The universal pH indicator paper was shown as color reference. All values are based on three biological repeats. The difference significances were done by one-way ANOVA and Duncan’s test at <italic toggle="yes">p</italic> &lt; 0.05, and the uppercase letters are used to show differences for C, while lowercase letters are used for ΔE and other indices.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="foods-13-00833-g002.jpg"><?image-name foods-13-00833-g002.jpg?><?image-size 188993?><?image-md5 444dee788b03c89e5cef2246b25ef00a?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 3718?><?image-original-width 3522?><?image-scaled-height 826?><?image-scaled-width 782?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/5d30/10969496/444dee788b03/foods-13-00833-g002.jpg?><?thumb-name foods-13-00833-g002.gif?><?thumb-size 8978?><?thumb-md5 de8772e51fdeeaa3adf0712cd9f41238?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 106?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/5d30/10969496/de8772e51fde/foods-13-00833-g002.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="foods-13-00833-f003" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Color and spectra for the diluted extracts by four solvents with theoretically identical initial PSPA concentration. (<bold>A</bold>) Color of PSPA solutions at 0–54 days’ storage in the dark under room temperature. For photographs of Day 0 and 25, plain white printing paper was used as the background. For Day 54, glossy white paper (generally used for packaging boxes) was selected, resulting in clearer and brighter photos. (<bold>B</bold>) Absorption spectra of PSPA solutions at Day 0.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="foods-13-00833-g003.jpg"><?image-name foods-13-00833-g003.jpg?><?image-size 106826?><?image-md5 0afb3608d663de734f376792db72294a?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2850?><?image-original-width 4076?><?image-scaled-height 518?><?image-scaled-width 741?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/5d30/10969496/0afb3608d663/foods-13-00833-g003.jpg?><?thumb-name foods-13-00833-g003.gif?><?thumb-size 8190?><?thumb-md5 9e924122d1c87240268eb1526a3fd319?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 80?><?thumb-scaled-width 114?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/5d30/10969496/9e924122d1c8/foods-13-00833-g003.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="foods-13-00833-f004" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>The joint impact of pH and temperature on the stability of PSPA solutions. (<bold>A</bold>) Color and (<bold>B</bold>) spectra of PSPA solutions at pH 1 to 13. The spectra of SD-w sample were shown and λmax for pH solutions were indicated in the brackets. Color change of PSPA solutions in dark at (<bold>C</bold>) 65 °C for 0–12 h, and at (<bold>D</bold>) 25 °C for 0–32 days.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="foods-13-00833-g004.jpg"><?image-name foods-13-00833-g004.jpg?><?image-size 155678?><?image-md5 6ab0b5ba2e82713261fb02d211a13c4b?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2423?><?image-original-width 3261?><?image-scaled-height 538?><?image-scaled-width 724?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/5d30/10969496/6ab0b5ba2e82/foods-13-00833-g004.jpg?><?thumb-name foods-13-00833-g004.gif?><?thumb-size 9330?><?thumb-md5 7fcb01dd658017ebd04a09eed0